  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1236 AM MDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nSunday...Upper low pushes up into east central UT through the day \nSunday, then weakens slightly and slides east into CO through the \nevening. Widespread showers and thunderstorms will continue across \nthe forecast area through the afternoon, but the steering flow \nshifts from a S-SW direction to a more W-SW direction through late \nafternoon, keeping much of the stronger activity and highest QPF \namounts right along the eastern border. The mt peaks may see an \nadditional 1-2 inches of new snow, though snowfall is expected to \nreally drop off. This should be the coolest day of the forecast \nperiod, with highs in the 50s for most locations, and nearing 60F \nfor the far eastern plains.\n\nMonday...Upper low weakens, opens and exits to the northeast through \nthe day, with just some lingering isolated to low end scattered \nshowers across the far eastern plains through the morning. Temps \nstart to warm back up to near seasonal levels, with highs in the 50s \nto near 60F for the high valleys, and 60s for the plains.\n\nTuesday through Friday...Broad southwest flow across the region will \nsettle in for the remainder of the week. A disturbance brushes by to \nthe north on Tuesday, producing some brisk winds, otherwise \nconditions remain dry and temps remain seasonal as the Pacific \nmoisture tap is temporarily shut down and moisture from the Gulf \nstarts creeping northward towards the TX and OK panhandles.\nMoore",
